ritize how do we  prioritize uh testing and it's not just  something that happened to us it  happened really all across  the united states  so the pandemic particularly in the year  2020 had significant impact on sexually  transmitted uh infections it did on hpv  it did on gonorrhea it did on syphilis  it did on chlamydia as well what you  guys are looking at really is um the  percentage of uh of tests compared to  the previous year uh particularly  positive tests and we can see  they're just way down  in the year 2020 um and all of that  really is due to  change in some behaviors uh in  particular change in going to see  doctors uh during the earlier parts of  the pandemic  so  we  attract our testing at our institution  during the uh pandemic we saw  significant decreases in volumes of many  of our tests across the year 2020  particularly when it came to sexually  transmitted infections we saw fewer  chlamydia gonorrhea tests we solved  fewer hiv tests we saw fewer uh  hepatitis b we saw fewer syphilis  tests we now have basically returned to  baseline or to what we expect to be but  there was a significant impact in the  year 2020 and it's uh likely an impact  that the patient populations uh will be  observing for years to come and  particularly for hpv  um  according to our sort of charting  we expected to see about 8 800 hpv tests  uh in the year 2020. we only saw 7 400  hpv tests  that is a significant decline we didn't  see any declines in the positivity rates  we just saw declines in the number of  people uh who are tested so  to some extent hpv um  was uh uh likely spreading undiagnosed  uh in our patient population during that  time period and that's just something  that we have to deal with on the back  end now  while our volumes weigh down the uh  volumes of uh screening uh for cancers  in california in general we're down by  about eighty percent um so uh um we uh  we believe our harbinger for the rest of  the united states meaning that uh  testing was d